DirectSummands -- decompositions of graded modules and coherent sheaves
***********************************************************************

Description
===========

This package implements algorithms for computing indecomposable summands of
finitely generated $R$-module $M$ over a $k$-algebra when $M$ is either a
homogeneous module over a (multi)graded ring with $k$ a field of arbitrary
characteristic, or when $R$ is local and $k$ a field of positive characteristic
contained in $\overline{\mathbb F_p}$.

Further, in the graded case, this package enables the computation of
indecomposable summands of coherent sheaves on subvarieties of the projective
space $\PP^n$ as well as other complete toric varieties, while in the local
case it enables the study of germs of inhomogeneous singularities in positive
characteristic.

 Primary methods:

  * *note directSummands: directSummands, -- compute the direct summands of a
    module or coherent sheaf
  * *note isIndecomposable: isIndecomposable, -- tests whether a module or
    coherent sheaf is indecomposable

 Supporting methods:

  * *note changeBaseField: changeBaseField, -- extend scalars for modules,
    matrices, and coherent sheaves
  * *note generalEndomorphism: generalEndomorphism, -- construct a generic
    degree-zero endomorphism
  * *note findProjectors: findProjectors, -- construct projectors on a
    homogeneous module
  * *note findIdempotents: findIdempotents, -- construct idempotent
    endomorphisms
  * *note findSplitInclusion: findSplitInclusion, -- construct a split
    inclusion of one module into another
  * *note isomorphismTally: isomorphismTally, -- group isomorphic modules or
    sheaves in a list
  * *note tallySummands: tallySummands, -- tally a list of modules up to
    isomorphism
  * *note potentialExtension: potentialExtension, -- suggest a field extension
    where splitting may become visible

 Featured applications:

  * *note Example_colon_ summands of the Frobenius pushforward: Example_co
    summands of the Frobenius pushforward,
  * *note Example_colon_ syzygies over Artinian rings: Example_co syzygies over
    Artinian rings,
  * *note Example_colon_ symbolic diagonalization: Example_co symbolic
    diagonalization,

The decomposition algorithms are randomized, so repeated runs may sample
different endomorphisms. Over a large enough field, this implementation is
often capable of completely decomposing a module in a single non-recursive
iteration. When a decomposition appears only after extending scalars, the
package can suggest or carry out a field extension via *note
potentialExtension: potentialExtension, and *note changeBaseField:
changeBaseField,.

The package also (temporarily) includes utilities for working with the
Frobenius map in positive characteristic: *note frobeniusMap: frobeniusMap,,
*note frobeniusRing: frobeniusRing,, *note frobeniusPullback:
frobeniusPullback,, *note frobeniusPushforward: frobeniusPushforward,, and
*note frobeniusTwist: frobeniusTwist,.  These make it possible to study
decompositions of Frobenius pushforwards of modules and sheaves, which is one
of the motivating applications.

As a sample computation, consider the splitting of the Frobenius pushforward of
the structure sheaf of an elliptic curve over $\mathbb F_7$ and after base
change to $\mathbb F_{49}$.

