isDirectSummand -- test whether one module is a direct summand of another
*************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        isDirectSummand(L, M)
  * Inputs:
      * L, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, the candidate summand
      * M, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, the ambient module
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Limit (missing documentation) => ..., default value null, 
      * Splitting
      * Strategy (missing documentation) => ..., default value 3, 
      * Tries (missing documentation) => ..., default value null, 
      * Verbose (missing documentation) => ..., default value false, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, whether the algorithm
        finds $L$ as a direct summand of $M$

Description
===========

This function tries to detect whether $L$ is a direct summand of $M$ by
searching for maps $L \to M$ and $M \to L$ whose composition is an isomorphism
of $L$.

The computation is randomized, so a return value of true certifies that a
summand was found, while a return value of false only means that no splitting
was found within the allotted number of attempts.  The alias isSummand is
provided for convenience.

When $L$ is free, the method instead searches for a surjection $M \to L$, which
is enough to split off a free summand.

See also
========

  * *note directSummands: directSummands, -- compute the direct summands of a
    module or coherent sheaf
  * *note findSplitInclusion: findSplitInclusion, -- construct a split
    inclusion of one module into another
  * *note isIndecomposable: isIndecomposable, -- tests whether a module or
    coherent sheaf is indecomposable

Ways to use isDirectSummand:
============================

  * "isDirectSummand(Module,Module)"

For the programmer
==================

